  <h2>Start with calories, not cups</h2>
  <p>
    Cat food labels list kilocalories per cup or per can, often as “ME” (metabolizable energy). Two
    brands can differ by 100 kcal per cup, so switching foods without recalculating is a common reason
    cats gain or lose weight unexpectedly. Veterinary nutrition starts from Resting Energy Requirement:
    about 70 × (weight in kg) to the power of 0.75. Multiplying RER by 1.0 to 1.2 often estimates
    maintenance for a lean indoor cat; overweight cats may need less under vet guidance; kittens and
    active outdoor cats need more.
  </p>
  <p>
    Enter your cat’s weight and lifestyle into the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/cat-food-calculator/">cat food calculator</a> to see a daily kcal range and sample
    portions for typical wet and dry products. Treat that number as a hypothesis: you prove or disprove
    it with a scale and your hands on the ribs over the next two to four weeks.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading labels the AAFCO and FDA way</h2>
  <p>
    Look for a nutritional adequacy statement such as “complete and balanced for adult maintenance” or
    “for growth and reproduction.” AAFCO sets the nutrient profiles behind those claims; FDA enforces
    truthful labeling and safe manufacturing. Marketing phrases like “premium,” “human grade,” or
    “grain free” do not by themselves tell you whether taurine, arachidonic acid, or vitamin D levels
    are correct. If you feed a homemade or raw diet, WSAVA guidelines recommend working with a
    veterinary nutritionist because cats fail quickly on imbalanced recipes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Life stage matters. Kitten food is denser in calories and nutrients; adult food is wrong for a
    growing kitten, and kitten food fed long term to a lazy adult can cause obesity. The{' '}
    <a href="/tools/cat-age-calculator/">cat age calculator</a> maps calendar age to life stages used in
    AAFP–AAHA guidelines, which helps you choose checkup frequency and feeding goals, not just food bag
    labels.
  </p>

  <h2>Wet versus dry: hydration and convenience</h2>
  <p>
    Cats evolved as desert hunters and have a weak thirst drive compared with dogs. Many drink too
    little when fed only dry food, which concentrates urine and may contribute to urinary tract issues
    in susceptible cats. Wet food is mostly water by weight, so each meal adds fluid without relying on
    the water bowl. If you prefer dry kibble for cost or convenience, place multiple fresh water
    stations, consider a fountain, and monitor urine clumps in the litter box for sudden changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neither texture wins on nutrition alone; a complete dry food beats an incomplete wet food and vice
    versa. Some owners mix wet and dry; subtract calories from both so the total still matches the
    target. Dental claims for dry food are modest—regular veterinary dental care still matters.
  </p>

  <h2>Meal schedules and free-feeding</h2>
  <p>
    Free-feeding dry food works for a few naturally lean cats, but most indoor cats overeat when the
    bowl is always full. Measured meals make appetite changes visible: a cat that normally finishes
    breakfast but leaves half may be ill. Kittens under six months usually need three to four meals per
    day to avoid energy dips. Adults often do well with morning and evening meals; late-night snacks
    should count toward the daily kcal budget, not sit outside it.
  </p>

  <h2>Body condition scoring at home</h2>
  <p>
    You should feel ribs with light pressure, see a waist behind the ribs when viewed from above, and
    notice a slight abdominal tuck from the side. Fat pads on the chest and a swinging belly often mean
    it is time to trim portions by 10 percent and recheck in two weeks. Obesity raises diabetes and
    arthritis risk in cats; weight loss must be gradual because rapid fasting can trigger hepatic
    lipidosis, a serious liver condition. Monthly weigh-ins on a baby scale or at the clinic keep
    progress honest.
  </p>

  <h2>Treats, toppers, and “just a bite” culture</h2>
  <p>
    Treats should stay near 10 percent of daily calories. That includes dental sticks, squeeze treats,
    and the crust of your sandwich. If training requires many rewards, use tiny pieces of the cat’s
    regular kibble or a low-calorie option and reduce meal size accordingly. Rich human foods—tuna packed
    for people, cream, deli meat—can unbalance the diet or upset the stomach. Onions, garlic, grapes,
    raisins, alcohol, and xylitol sweetener are dangerous for cats as well as dogs.
  </p>

  <h2>Life stages in practice</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Kittens</strong> need growth formulas until about 12 months, though large breeds like Maine
    Coons may benefit from kitten food longer while they fill out—see the{' '}
    <a href="/guides/kitten-feeding-schedule/">kitten feeding schedule</a> for meals-per-day by age.
    <strong>Adult cats</strong> from about 1 to 6 or 7 years should stay lean to protect joints and
    metabolism. <strong>Mature and senior cats</strong> may need more protein to preserve muscle, not
    less, unless a veterinarian prescribes a phosphorus-restricted renal diet. Screen older cats for
    hyperthyroidism, kidney disease, and diabetes; all three change appetite and weight abruptly. For
    the full AAFP/AAHA-style band chart, read <a href="/guides/cat-life-stages/">cat life stages</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pregnant and nursing queens need kitten food and free access to calories until kittens wean; this
    is one of the few times unlimited feeding is appropriate, and even then obesity before pregnancy
    should be corrected first under vet advice.
  </p>

  <h2>Multi-cat households</h2>
  <p>
    Cats differ in metabolism. Feeding one communal bowl lets the food-motivated cat become round while
    the shy cat stays thin. Separate feeding stations, microchip-activated feeders, or timed meals in
    closed rooms let each cat eat its prescription or calorie target. Overweight cats should not be
    starved while others graze; separate rooms for weight-loss plans are kinder and safer.
  </p>

  <h2>When to call the veterinarian</h2>
  <p>
    Sudden hunger with weight loss, drinking more water, vomiting, or refusing food for more than 24
    hours warrants an appointment. Cats hide illness; a half-pound drop in a 10 lb cat is significant.
    Bring your calorie math and brand name to the visit so the team can adjust therapeutics without
    guessing. WSAVA nutrition guidelines emphasize that therapeutic diets for kidney, urinary, or
    allergy disease should be chosen with professional diagnosis, not internet speculation.
  </p>

  <h2>Bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Good cat feeding is boring in the best way: a complete food, a measured kcal target, hydration,
    and regular body checks. Use AAFCO statements and FDA label literacy to pick the product, use our
    calculator to set portions, and use your vet to interpret weight trends and lab work. Cats live long
    when they stay lean, hydrated, and fed a diet that respects their carnivore chemistry—not when they
    inherit the largest bowl in the pantry.
  </p>
